@anon.53fe85e3a453dfbf0ab1e8743506e67c.1431 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [4152 x i8] }> <{ [4152 x i8] c"Represents a date, a time, or a combination of both.\0A\0ACan be created by either specifying a custom datetime using this type's\0Aconstructor function or getting the current date with\0A[`datetime.today`]($datetime.today).\0A\0A# Example\0A```example\0A#let date = datetime(\0A  year: 2020,\0A  month: 10,\0A  day: 4,\0A)\0A\0A#date.display() \\\0A#date.display(\0A  \22y:[year repr:last_two]\22\0A)\0A\0A#let time = datetime(\0A  hour: 18,\0A  minute: 2,\0A  second: 23,\0A)\0A\0A#time.display() \\\0A#time.display(\0A  \22h:[hour repr:12][period]\22\0A)\0A```\0A\0A# Format\0AYou can specify a customized formatting using the\0A[`display`]($datetime.display) method. The format of a datetime is\0Aspecified by providing _components_ with a specified number of _modifiers_.\0AA component represents a certain part of the datetime that you want to\0Adisplay, and with the help of modifiers you can define how you want to\0Adisplay that component. In order to display a component, you wrap the name\0Aof the component in square brackets (e.g. `[[year]]` will display the year).\0AIn order to add modifiers, you add a space after the component name followed\0Aby the name of the modifier, a colon and the value of the modifier (e.g.\0A`[[month repr:short]]` will display the short representation of the month).\0A\0AThe possible combination of components and their respective modifiers is as\0Afollows:\0A\0A- `year`: Displays the year of the datetime.\0A  - `padding`: Can be either `zero`, `space` or `none`. Specifies how the\0A    year is padded.\0A  - `repr` Can be either `full` in which case the full year is displayed or\0A    `last_two` in which case only the last two digits are displayed.\0A  - `sign`: Can be either `automatic` or `mandatory`. Specifies when the\0A    sign should be displayed.\0A- `month`: Displays the month of the datetime.\0A  - `padding`: Can be either `zero`, `space` or `none`. Specifies how the\0A    month is padded.\0A  - `repr`: Can be either `numerical`, `long` or `short`. Specifies if the\0A    month should be displayed as a number or a word. Unfortunately, when\0A    choosing the word representation, it can currently only display the\0A    English version. In the future, it is planned to support localization.\0A- `day`: Displays the day of the datetime.\0A  - `padding`: Can be either `zero`, `space` or `none`. Specifies how the\0A    day is padded.\0A- `week_number`: Displays the week number of the datetime.\0A  - `padding`: Can be either `zero`, `space` or `none`. Specifies how the\0A    week number is padded.\0A  - `repr`: Can be either `ISO`, `sunday` or `monday`. In the case of `ISO`,\0A     week numbers are between 1 and 53, while the other ones are between 0\0A     and 53.\0A- `weekday`: Displays the weekday of the date.\0A  - `repr` Can be either `long`, `short`, `sunday` or `monday`. In the case\0A    of `long` and `short`, the corresponding English name will be displayed\0A    (same as for the month, other languages are currently not supported). In\0A    the case of `sunday` and `monday`, the numerical value will be displayed\0A    (assuming Sunday and Monday as the first day of the week, respectively).\0A  - `one_indexed`: Can be either `true` or `false`. Defines whether the\0A    numerical representation of the week starts with 0 or 1.\0A- `hour`: Displays the hour of the date.\0A  - `padding`: Can be either `zero`, `space` or `none`. Specifies how the\0A    hour is padded.\0A  - `repr`: Can be either `24` or `12`. Changes whether the hour is\0A    displayed in the 24-hour or 12-hour format.\0A- `period`: The AM/PM part of the hour\0A  - `case`: Can be `lower` to display it in lower case and `upper` to\0A    display it in upper case.\0A- `minute`: Displays the minute of the date.\0A  - `padding`: Can be either `zero`, `space` or `none`. Specifies how the\0A    minute is padded.\0A- `second`: Displays the second of the date.\0A  - `padding`: Can be either `zero`, `space` or `none`. Specifies how the\0A    second is padded.\0A\0AKeep in mind that not always all components can be used. For example, if you\0Acreate a new datetime with `{datetime(year: 2023, month: 10, day: 13)}`, it\0Awill be stored as a plain date internally, meaning that you cannot use\0Acomponents such as `hour` or `minute`, which would only work on datetimes\0Athat have a specified time." }>, align 1
